Summary: NetHack intimidating to the novice?

I was messing around the other day, looking through the lists of games
available from our local machine besides NetHack, and I noticed a game called
Moria.  I've heard it's fairly similar to NetHack in some respects, so I
decided to give it a try.

Whoa.

I couldn't figure out what to do; whether my weapons were even ready, much
less being used; and it looked like I was in some sort of city!  It was a
very confusing experience, and I quit quickly, to return to NetHack.

Then it occured to me: I was eased into the current complexities of NetHack.
I played Rogue in high school, then got an older version of NetHack (2.plugh),
which I had my best character ever on (in the days when spells didn't get worn
out, Dig and Force Bolt are your friends!) even if I didn't finish it (I didn't
know about needing to use a level teleporter).  Then I went to 3.0 when I got
into college, and have followed the versions since (well, actually, I'm still
on 3.1.1; I'll have to chat with the guy who runs the games account.)  I would
imagine that anyone who *hadn't* had that gradual introduction to the program
would be rather intimidated by the whole thing, wouldn't you?  Maybe not want
to play it again?

Thus, the idea: to write a newbie's guide to NetHack.  While the game's
internal help makes a great reference guide, it isn't a good tutorial; I
think I could write a reasonably entertaining document to help people new to
the game get into it.  (Who knows?  It might even make the standard
distribution!  (;3)

I thought I'd poll you folks out there, for your advice, comments, et cetera.
I don't want to do this if it's been done, and done well, before (though if it
has, I certainly haven't heard of it, and no doubt neither have the newbies);
neither do I want to replicate spoiler file efforts.  My aim is to getting the
person into the game, and letting them figure out the mind puzzles, while
putting the controls easily at their fingertips.

Ideas for what to put in include:

* The basics: how to quit the game and how to get help.  Those are the first
  things -I- want to know when running a new program.

* Basic actions: walking around, fighting things, searching.

* Saving and restoring games, and the nature of the saved game.

* Objects: what they look like, what they do, how to use them.

* Your pet, and what it's good for.

* And perhaps a few rumors, to set the player off on the right foot.  (Stuff
  like the existence of Vlad's Tower, the Gnomish Mines, et cetera.)

So... what do you think?

